{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-143779",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,1).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{4}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
